I want to be repped by athlete.

763
00:34:02.780 --> 00:34:03.740
Oh, I'm sorry, man.

764
00:34:03.820 --> 00:34:05.120
We only represent athletes.

765
00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:07.420
That's what the F stands for.

766
00:34:09.760 --> 00:34:12.960
Oh, so you just get more fame and wealth for people who already have it.

767
00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:13.960
That's the idea.

768
00:34:15.120 --> 00:34:17.680
Well, it's a little like shooting fish in a barrel, don't you think?

769
00:34:18.580 --> 00:34:20.360
You know, we have a board meeting tomorrow.

770
00:34:21.060 --> 00:34:24.980
I can't make any promises, but I'm pretty sure I won't bring it up.

771
00:34:26.120 --> 00:34:27.360
But you can't make any promises.

772
00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:28.400
Can't make any promises.

773
00:34:29.180 --> 00:34:30.380
I'm just going to say this.

774
00:34:30.460 --> 00:34:31.760
You can do with it whatever you want.

775
00:34:31.860 --> 00:34:32.240
Of course.

776
00:34:32.300 --> 00:34:34.660
On my acting resume under special skills.

777
00:34:34.780 --> 00:34:34.940
Yeah.

778
00:34:35.920 --> 00:34:37.140
Fencing and tetherball.

779
00:34:37.680 --> 00:34:38.900
Those are sports.

780
00:34:39.280 --> 00:34:40.720
So just think about it.

781
00:34:41.060 --> 00:34:41.320
Okay.

782
00:34:41.680 --> 00:34:43.280
I'm going to write that down.

783
00:34:43.600 --> 00:34:43.860
Cool.

784
00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:44.660
Let's see.

785
00:34:46.340 --> 00:34:49.580
I enjoyed that so much.

786
00:34:49.620 --> 00:34:53.360
I just love that he put tetherball on his special skill.

787
00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:57.200
Do kids today even play tetherball?

788
00:34:57.640 --> 00:34:59.040
They're at a few parks.

789
00:34:59.040 --> 00:35:00.360
I've seen them here or there.

790
00:35:00.620 --> 00:35:00.880
Yeah?

791
00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:01.380
Yeah.

792
00:35:01.380 --> 00:35:01.840
Okay.

793
00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:06.560
That was like very exciting if the park had a tetherball when I was a kid.

794
00:35:06.740 --> 00:35:11.040
I was always absolutely destroyed in tetherball because I'm short.

795
00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:11.880
I'm short.

796
00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:12.460
Yeah.

797
00:35:12.640 --> 00:35:14.580
I once got hit in the face with a tetherball.

798
00:35:14.620 --> 00:35:14.840
Oh, no.

799
00:35:14.960 --> 00:35:16.800
I don't have good memories of tetherball.

800
00:35:16.860 --> 00:35:17.920
It doesn't sound like it.

801
00:35:17.920 --> 00:35:22.380
Well, now Andy's on hold with another talent agent and Dwight enters.

802
00:35:22.380 --> 00:35:24.260
He's complaining about Stanley.

803
00:35:24.640 --> 00:35:30.440
And basically in this scene, Andy is going to give Dwight permission to get Stanley to

804
00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:33.220
this sales call by any means possible.

805
00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:34.300
Yeah.

806
00:35:34.620 --> 00:35:39.180
And, you know, Dwight's going to have a talking head that says for five years that he's held

807
00:35:39.180 --> 00:35:42.080
his instincts in check because he wanted to be manager.

808
00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:44.880
But now it's time for him to just let that go.

809
00:35:44.880 --> 00:35:46.000
And it's really freeing.

810
00:35:46.160 --> 00:35:50.820
He's going to follow his instincts now, which basically involves getting a bull tranquilizer

811
00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:52.920
and drugging Stanley to get him to this meeting.

812
00:35:53.180 --> 00:35:53.540
Yeah.

813
00:35:53.680 --> 00:35:55.100
Dwight walks into the break room.

814
00:35:55.240 --> 00:35:56.280
Clark is standing there.

815
00:35:56.440 --> 00:36:00.060
Clark has one of my favorite lines in the episode, in this scene.

816
00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:00.580
What?

817
00:36:00.580 --> 00:36:05.360
When Dwight says, Stanley, one way or another, you're going to come with me to make this sale.

818
00:36:05.600 --> 00:36:10.280
Stanley goes past and then Clark says, hey, can you just let me out of here before whatever

819
00:36:10.280 --> 00:36:11.040
comes next?

820
00:36:11.500 --> 00:36:11.780
Yeah.

821
00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:18.900
Because Dwight's got this ridiculously long tranquilizer gun and he shoots Stanley three

822
00:36:18.900 --> 00:36:19.520
times.

823
00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:21.240
With little red dart things.

824
00:36:21.520 --> 00:36:22.780
And then Stanley collapses.

825
00:36:23.600 --> 00:36:26.800
And Dwight says to Clark, don't worry, Andy approved this.

826
00:36:27.400 --> 00:36:30.140
Well, we got a lot of mail about this, as you can imagine.

827
00:36:30.140 --> 00:36:30.980
David H.

828
00:36:31.120 --> 00:36:35.340
from England said, how did you create the illusion of Stanley being shot with darts?

829
00:36:35.660 --> 00:36:37.100
And Lily B.G.

830
00:36:37.300 --> 00:36:41.840
from Canada said, what was used for the bull tranquilizers when Dwight shoots

831
00:36:41.840 --> 00:36:42.280
Stanley?

832
00:36:42.560 --> 00:36:45.660
Well, Steve Burgess said it was visual effects.

833
00:36:46.260 --> 00:36:49.860
We did not actually shoot anything at Leslie David Baker.

834
00:36:50.900 --> 00:36:52.360
We filmed the dart.

835
00:36:52.580 --> 00:36:53.680
We filmed Stanley.

836
00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:56.240
We attached the dart to his clothes.

837
00:36:56.400 --> 00:37:02.480
And then in post-production, it looks like the darts going into him.

838
00:37:02.660 --> 00:37:03.040
Oh, yeah.

839
00:37:03.060 --> 00:37:04.320
It was all visual effects.

840
00:37:04.900 --> 00:37:09.460
As far as the moment when Leslie is tranquilized, Matt Soane said the first thing they had to

841
00:37:09.460 --> 00:37:11.320
figure out was this dart gun.

842
00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:12.400
Here's what he said.

843
00:37:12.400 --> 00:37:22.120
We had a lot of conversations about the dart gun to make it feel as much of a benign weapon

844
00:37:22.120 --> 00:37:22.880
as possible.

845
00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:27.240
We didn't want a real gun or a rifle for Dwight to have.

846
00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:34.340
So we sort of concocted this version of what this dart gun was.

847
00:37:34.460 --> 00:37:36.260
And that was what we came up with.

848
00:37:37.240 --> 00:37:41.400
Yeah, Steve said we went through a few different dart gun options.

849
00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:47.260
The network had given a note that they didn't want this to look like an actual weapon.

850
00:37:47.680 --> 00:37:50.460
And so Phil Shea found this one that we used.

851
00:37:50.540 --> 00:37:52.480
It looked kind of like a dart shooter.

852
00:37:52.680 --> 00:37:55.420
It had that really long, kind of like skinny barrel.

853
00:37:55.420 --> 00:37:56.780
It was very cartoonish.

854
00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:57.820
Yeah, exactly.

855
00:37:58.700 --> 00:38:02.300
Matt Soane also talked about how Leslie really sold this moment.

856
00:38:02.300 --> 00:38:03.620
I thought he did fantastic.

857
00:38:03.640 --> 00:38:04.960
Let's hear what Matt said.

858
00:38:05.740 --> 00:38:12.380
Leslie did an amazing collapse and fall into a pad that we had set up.

859
00:38:12.540 --> 00:38:17.120
And we did it a couple times, but he really enjoyed the moment and had fun with it.

860
00:38:17.240 --> 00:38:21.480
I feel like for this episode, this was something that was very different for Leslie.

861
00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:30.700
And again, it gave us a fun moment to see Rain and Clark Duke have another buddy moment like

862
00:38:30.700 --> 00:38:32.580
they did in Suit Warehouse.

863
00:38:32.580 --> 00:38:38.480
I think that Clark's reaction in the break room as all this is going down was great.

864
00:38:38.920 --> 00:38:42.700
Well, I agree that Leslie was excellent in the scene.

865
00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:44.200
I mean, when he falls forward.

866
00:38:44.600 --> 00:38:44.840
Yeah.

867
00:38:45.100 --> 00:38:48.540
You don't see him like brace himself with his hands or anything.

868
00:38:48.800 --> 00:38:49.960
I mean, that is hard to do.

869
00:38:49.960 --> 00:38:58.600
This is now going to set off a dilemma for Dwight, which is how does he get Stanley from the break room

870
00:38:58.600 --> 00:39:02.860
on the floor all the way down to the car and into a meeting?

871
00:39:03.360 --> 00:39:05.920
So maybe we should describe how he does that.

872
00:39:06.360 --> 00:39:06.720
Mm-hmm.

873
00:39:06.980 --> 00:39:10.780
He starts by somehow getting Clark to help him.

874
00:39:11.600 --> 00:39:11.820
Yeah.

875
00:39:12.020 --> 00:39:12.960
Clark witnessed this.

876
00:39:12.960 --> 00:39:13.880
He's in it now.

877
00:39:14.220 --> 00:39:14.520
Yeah.

878
00:39:14.840 --> 00:39:17.900
They're trying to move him by hand and it is not working.

879
00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:18.300
Mm-mm.

880
00:39:19.160 --> 00:39:21.100
So they get a rolly office chair.

881
00:39:21.480 --> 00:39:25.860
I also want to give props to Leslie David Baker for his acting in the rolly chair.

882
00:39:26.260 --> 00:39:26.820
Oh, yeah.

883
00:39:27.340 --> 00:39:28.080
All of it.

884
00:39:28.200 --> 00:39:32.700
He had to just be out of it while they're tugging him and having dialogue around him.

885
00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:33.900
I thought he did fantastic.

886
00:39:34.260 --> 00:39:35.520
The rolly chair works.

887
00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:37.300
But remember, the elevator's broken.

888
00:39:37.300 --> 00:39:39.040
How are they going to get him down the stairs?

889
00:39:39.040 --> 00:39:39.700
Yeah.

890
00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:47.080
What happens is I guess Dwight makes a cardboard ramp and now they need to wrap Stanley in bubble wrap

891
00:39:47.080 --> 00:39:49.380
and they're going to strap a helmet onto his head.

892
00:39:49.700 --> 00:39:52.620
This looks like something out of that movie Jackass.

893
00:39:52.860 --> 00:39:54.040
It really does.

894
00:39:54.440 --> 00:39:59.480
And while they're discussing the best way to kind of slide him down the stairs,

895
00:39:59.480 --> 00:40:02.340
Stanley slides down the stairs on his own.

896
00:40:02.400 --> 00:40:03.160
By accident.

897
00:40:03.480 --> 00:40:05.340
And his head goes into the wall.

898
00:40:05.780 --> 00:40:06.900
And leaves a divot.

899
00:40:06.900 --> 00:40:09.880
But eventually they get him down to the parking lot and into the car.

900
00:40:10.160 --> 00:40:11.540
We had a fan mail flurry.

901
00:40:11.740 --> 00:40:12.340
I bet.

902
00:40:12.860 --> 00:40:16.920
Basically, people said, please give us all the details on the Stanley stunts.

903
00:40:17.160 --> 00:40:21.840
What stunts did Leslie David Baker do versus the ones a stunt double did?

904
00:40:22.640 --> 00:40:25.840
Well, lucky for us, Matt Stone broke it all down.

905
00:40:25.840 --> 00:40:27.200
And here's what he had to say.

906
00:40:27.200 --> 00:40:34.700
We have moments where we used the real Stanley, but we also had a stuntman playing Stanley.

907
00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:40.800
And it was the stuntman whose head we sort of ran into the wall in the break room.

908
00:40:41.000 --> 00:40:50.460
But Leslie did all of his own stuff when they were dropping him into the chair and pushing him down the hall.

909
00:40:50.460 --> 00:40:59.900
When it came to the stairwell slide, we had discussed many different options on how to do this.

910
00:41:00.280 --> 00:41:05.740
We had the stuntman, so we talked about using the stuntman to slide down.

911
00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:11.800
But the problem was that it was too dangerous of a slide because whoever was going to do it,

912
00:41:11.900 --> 00:41:15.040
their arms were trapped in the bubble wrap by their side.

913
00:41:15.040 --> 00:41:23.680
So we ultimately figured it was the safest to build a dummy to slide down the ramp and hit the wall,

914
00:41:23.720 --> 00:41:29.060
which is ultimately what we did and managed to hide Stanley's face.

915
00:41:29.280 --> 00:41:41.580
And then we have the cutaway after the fact of Leslie wrapped up in the helmet by the hole that we punched in the wall.

916
00:41:41.580 --> 00:41:48.260
Now, getting Stanley into the car, Leslie played great at being passed out.

917
00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:55.920
And, you know, it was a challenge for both Clark and Rain because Leslie didn't have a lot of control

918
00:41:55.920 --> 00:41:58.880
because he was in the bubble wrap and his arms were by his side.

919
00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:04.680
And they had to sort of maneuver him around and attempt to get him into the car.

920
00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:08.860
We did that in a lot of edits as well, which kind of sold the moment.

921
00:42:10.140 --> 00:42:11.900
I have to say, I thought it was seamless.

922
00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:12.980
Yeah.

923
00:42:13.420 --> 00:42:16.480
I mean, it was a dummy.

924
00:42:16.780 --> 00:42:17.160
Right.

925
00:42:17.300 --> 00:42:18.960
A stunt double and Leslie.

926
00:42:19.260 --> 00:42:20.300
They did a great job.

927
00:42:20.600 --> 00:42:21.380
And I could never tell.

928
00:42:21.640 --> 00:42:21.780
Yeah.

929
00:42:22.260 --> 00:42:25.120
Steve said Leslie's stunt double was Buddy Sawstand.

930
00:42:25.340 --> 00:42:27.660
Brett Jones was our stunt coordinator for this episode.

931
00:42:27.980 --> 00:42:31.820
I got a little curious about the Stanley dummy that Matt talked about.

932
00:42:32.060 --> 00:42:34.000
So I reached out to Steve Burgess.

933
00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:40.160
Because I was like, did Leslie have to do that thing where you get like a face cast made?

934
00:42:40.300 --> 00:42:43.000
Or, you know, you've seen that before, right?

935
00:42:43.560 --> 00:42:46.840
Steve Burgess said Phil actually used a dummy that he already had.

936
00:42:47.000 --> 00:42:52.740
And since it was wrapped up in bubble wrap and wearing a helmet, it kind of worked.

937
00:42:52.900 --> 00:42:53.780
It sold it.

938
00:42:53.900 --> 00:42:57.860
So no, Leslie did not have to sit through some 12-hour molding of his face.

939
00:42:58.880 --> 00:43:02.840
Obviously, all the shots where we see a face are Leslie.

940
00:43:03.220 --> 00:43:05.700
The slide down the stairs is the dummy.

941
00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:10.640
But then when we cut back to the dummy at the end of the stairs, that was Leslie again.

942
00:43:10.920 --> 00:43:12.200
It was just amazing.

943
00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:18.040
We did get another fan question about this sequence, Angela, from Jenna F.

944
00:43:18.140 --> 00:43:18.880
in Los Angeles.

945
00:43:19.460 --> 00:43:20.820
She writes in a lot.

946
00:43:20.820 --> 00:43:33.700
Yeah, she noticed that Rain slid down the cardboard and really felt like this was something Rain might have done on the fly that made everyone freak out.

947
00:43:34.280 --> 00:43:36.760
I asked Matt Soane about it, and here's what he said.

948
00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:37.880
Are you the Jenna F.

949
00:43:37.980 --> 00:43:38.680
in Los Angeles?

950
00:43:39.300 --> 00:43:40.420
It's a coincidence.

951
00:43:42.460 --> 00:43:45.760
Rain really wanted to slide down the stairs.

952
00:43:46.520 --> 00:43:54.800
And I feel like production told him not to, but he did it anyway, and it was sort of within the scene.

953
00:43:55.060 --> 00:43:56.020
It was very funny.

954
00:43:56.240 --> 00:44:02.920
But again, production wants to keep the actors safe, and it was a long slide.

955
00:44:03.540 --> 00:44:04.620
Rain did the slide.

956
00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:05.940
I think he enjoyed it.

957
00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:11.260
I think he did it maybe twice, but I'm not sure it could have also been our last take.

958
00:44:11.260 --> 00:44:15.540
Because I feel like we had discussed him sliding down, and he was told not to.

959
00:44:16.300 --> 00:44:22.140
But he did it anyway, and it made the cut, and it was very funny and felt realistic.

960
00:44:24.640 --> 00:44:24.800
Yeah.

961
00:44:25.260 --> 00:44:28.060
Steve Burgess also confirmed that Rain did it on the fly.

962
00:44:28.400 --> 00:44:36.640
Well, I was filming over on that side, you know, with the senator in the parking lot, and I distinctly remember our crew guys sliding down it.

963
00:44:36.860 --> 00:44:42.860
Steve Burgess said that that was why they weren't that concerned when Rain did it, because the crew had been doing it all day.

964
00:44:43.020 --> 00:44:43.220
Yep.

965
00:44:44.060 --> 00:44:50.740
Well, why don't we take a break, and when we come back, we're going to get some real therapy session for Jim and Pam.

966
00:44:51.000 --> 00:44:53.220
Yeah, couples therapy when we're back.

967
00:44:53.460 --> 00:44:53.620
Mm-hmm.

968
00:45:04.500 --> 00:45:08.220
I'd like to start by saying I finally feel normal again.

969
00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:09.020
Oh.

970
00:45:09.560 --> 00:45:11.680
From the nasty drink that we drank.

971
00:45:11.860 --> 00:45:12.180
Mm-hmm.

972
00:45:12.360 --> 00:45:12.640
Mm-hmm.

973
00:45:12.960 --> 00:45:16.160
And I know we said it last week, I love our space.

974
00:45:17.060 --> 00:45:25.260
When I was coming in today from the car, I had an encounter, and it was so charming, and it just made me love our new studio even more.

975
00:45:25.260 --> 00:45:26.240
What was it?

976
00:45:26.740 --> 00:45:31.220
Okay, so, you know, we're located in a little bit of a touristy area.

977
00:45:31.400 --> 00:45:32.020
We are.

978
00:45:32.620 --> 00:45:38.940
And there was a family on the street, and they were looking at their phone, and they looked like tourists to me.

979
00:45:39.160 --> 00:45:39.420
Right.

980
00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:43.320
And I thought, oh, I'm going to say hi to them.

981
00:45:43.460 --> 00:45:45.100
I'm going to ask them if they need directions.

982
00:45:45.480 --> 00:45:46.840
Because they looked a little loSt.

983
00:45:46.940 --> 00:45:47.460
A little bit.

984
00:45:47.560 --> 00:45:47.860
Mm-hmm.

985
00:45:47.860 --> 00:45:51.920
So I said, hey, are you traveling?

986
00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:53.080
Are you tourists?

987
00:45:53.520 --> 00:45:54.800
And they looked at me for a second.

988
00:45:54.880 --> 00:45:55.640
They kind of paused.

989
00:45:55.680 --> 00:45:59.400
And then they explained that they were French, that they didn't really speak English.

990
00:45:59.600 --> 00:46:01.320
And I said, oh.

991
00:46:01.860 --> 00:46:02.200
Bonjour.

992
00:46:02.560 --> 00:46:03.400
I said, bonjour.

993
00:46:03.640 --> 00:46:07.420
Did all of a sudden your French come back to you like it did in that market in France?

994
00:46:07.560 --> 00:46:08.260
I was hoping.

995
00:46:08.600 --> 00:46:10.540
And I said, je parle un peu de français.

996
00:46:10.940 --> 00:46:13.240
And she said, I speak a little English.

997
00:46:13.460 --> 00:46:16.120
And then I said, très un peu de français.

998
00:46:16.180 --> 00:46:17.360
Very little French.

999
00:46:17.360 --> 00:46:20.240
And the man said, un petit de français.

1000
00:46:20.900 --> 00:46:25.040
And I said, oui, petit de français, not très un peu.

1001
00:46:25.740 --> 00:46:27.800
But anyway, we had a moment.

1002
00:46:27.940 --> 00:46:29.300
They were looking for the Walk of Fame.

1003
00:46:29.480 --> 00:46:33.080
I was able to give them directions using au gauche and au droit.

1004
00:46:33.400 --> 00:46:34.840
I was very proud of myself.

1005
00:46:35.120 --> 00:46:37.940
And then I did a crazy thing, lady.

1006
00:46:38.080 --> 00:46:38.720
Is this crazy?

1007
00:46:39.200 --> 00:46:42.900
I said, do y'all watch The Office in France?

1008
00:46:43.160 --> 00:46:44.240
You did not.

1009
00:46:44.360 --> 00:46:44.860
I did.

1010
00:46:45.020 --> 00:46:46.240
This is not like you.

1011
00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:48.660
You are Miss Fly under the radar.

1012
00:46:49.040 --> 00:46:49.620
I know.

1013
00:46:49.620 --> 00:46:54.460
But I'm like, if you're a tourist and you've come from France and you're walking to the

1014
00:46:54.460 --> 00:47:00.140
Hollywood Walk of Fame and you've run into an actor or an actress, don't you want a picture

1015
00:47:00.140 --> 00:47:00.920
with that person?

1016
00:47:01.200 --> 00:47:01.940
Maybe so.

1017
00:47:02.060 --> 00:47:02.520
What happened?

1018
00:47:02.520 --> 00:47:06.460
They don't watch The Office, seem to have never heard of it.

1019
00:47:06.460 --> 00:47:08.240
They think you work in an office.

1020
00:47:08.500 --> 00:47:10.800
So, I kid you not.

1021
00:47:10.800 --> 00:47:16.500
I doubled down and I Googled myself and I showed my phone to them.

1022
00:47:16.740 --> 00:47:17.220
Jenna Fisher.

1023
00:47:17.220 --> 00:47:18.640
I said, this is me.

1024
00:47:18.800 --> 00:47:20.320
Who are you right now?

1025
00:47:20.320 --> 00:47:22.180
And they said, Jenna Fisher.

1026
00:47:22.780 --> 00:47:25.040
And I said, yes, that's me.

1027
00:47:25.240 --> 00:47:25.900
I'm Jenna Fisher.

1028
00:47:26.300 --> 00:47:30.120
And then they got all excited and the two girls, they were like kind of teen girls.

1029
00:47:30.320 --> 00:47:32.440
They wanted a picture and we took a picture.

1030
00:47:32.680 --> 00:47:36.420
And I could tell as they were walking away that they were like making a note to themselves.

1031
00:47:36.740 --> 00:47:37.920
We've met Jenna Fisher.

1032
00:47:38.220 --> 00:47:39.340
We don't know who she is.

1033
00:47:39.500 --> 00:47:42.560
She might be a famous receptioniSt.

1034
00:47:42.560 --> 00:47:43.900
Well, we don't know.

1035
00:47:45.060 --> 00:47:47.560
But that was my little encounter on the way in.

1036
00:47:47.720 --> 00:47:48.880
That's so charming.

1037
00:47:49.020 --> 00:47:49.620
I don't know.

1038
00:47:50.780 --> 00:47:52.600
I can't believe you did that.

1039
00:47:52.720 --> 00:47:57.120
Well, one of my favorite things to do is when we're driving somewhere and one of those like

1040
00:47:57.120 --> 00:48:03.060
tour buses, those like open air vans of tourists drive by and you're kind of at a stoplight

1041
00:48:03.060 --> 00:48:08.640
together, I always like to roll down my window and be like, hey, everybody, welcome to L.A.

1042
00:48:08.640 --> 00:48:14.140
Well, what I like to do is when I'm on vacation and I see a couple and they're also on vacation

1043
00:48:14.140 --> 00:48:18.360
and they're trying to do that thing that couples do where you take a selfie in front of some

1044
00:48:18.360 --> 00:48:21.960
beautiful thing, but you don't have someone to take it for you and you don't want to ask

1045
00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:22.740
someone maybe.

1046
00:48:23.480 --> 00:48:28.120
I go up to that couple or family and I say, do you guys want a group picture?

1047
00:48:28.240 --> 00:48:29.040
I'll take it for you.

1048
00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:31.560
And that's my favorite thing to do.

1049
00:48:31.700 --> 00:48:35.440
And sometimes people recognize me and sometimes they don't, but I don't ever say anything.

1050
00:48:35.760 --> 00:48:38.620
You don't get out your phone and Google yourself and explain who you are.

1051
00:48:38.640 --> 00:48:43.140
I don't, but it's always fun when that moment happens.

1052
00:48:43.720 --> 00:48:45.620
I just remember coming to L.A.

1053
00:48:45.760 --> 00:48:50.000
for the first time with my family and my sister and I think

1054
00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:56.620
I just saw myself in this family, you know, and how much I just wanted to run into some

1055
00:48:56.620 --> 00:48:58.500
person from film and television.

1056
00:48:58.780 --> 00:49:02.040
If Julia Roberts had been like, do you need help with directions?

1057
00:49:02.380 --> 00:49:03.460
Well, you know who we ran into?

1058
00:49:04.080 --> 00:49:05.080
Diane Keaton.

1059
00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:06.260
You and your family.

1060
00:49:06.260 --> 00:49:11.020
We passed her on the street and my mom is convinced to this day that Diane Keaton admired

1061
00:49:11.020 --> 00:49:11.560
her hat.

1062
00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:14.280
Family story.

1063
00:49:14.480 --> 00:49:15.340
It's legend.

1064
00:49:15.620 --> 00:49:15.740
Yeah.

1065
00:49:15.860 --> 00:49:19.040
Remember that time Diane Keaton did a double take?

1066
00:49:19.320 --> 00:49:20.580
Because she loved my hat.

1067
00:49:20.580 --> 00:49:21.220
She saw my hat.

1068
00:49:23.000 --> 00:49:24.780
Well, where should we go?

1069
00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:31.340
This very intense conversation is happening between Pam and Nellie and Jim and Toby.

1070
00:49:31.940 --> 00:49:33.080
We're cutting back and forth.

1071
00:49:33.380 --> 00:49:35.840
They're really sharing a lot of stuff, too.

1072
00:49:36.040 --> 00:49:39.540
Like, I'm just like, wow, this therapy session is going to be a big one.

1073
00:49:40.080 --> 00:49:40.300
Yeah.

1074
00:49:40.480 --> 00:49:46.340
I mean, Pam kind of says, okay, Jim did this without asking me, this without asking me.

1075
00:49:46.400 --> 00:49:50.520
At what point does he stop getting rewarded for making big life decisions without me?

1076
00:49:50.520 --> 00:49:55.700
And then Jim says, you know what, if I didn't usher in these life moments, Pam would still

1077
00:49:55.700 --> 00:49:56.600
be married to Roy.

1078
00:49:57.260 --> 00:50:00.180
There's someone who has a real issue with that statement from Jim.

1079
00:50:00.340 --> 00:50:00.620
Oh.

1080
00:50:01.080 --> 00:50:03.040
And it is Tanisha T from the UK.

1081
00:50:03.500 --> 00:50:04.540
Tanisha, let's hear it.

1082
00:50:05.080 --> 00:50:09.400
Tanisha says, I have a small rant and I've been waiting to get to this episode because

1083
00:50:09.400 --> 00:50:14.400
it has always made me so mad when Jim suggests that if he didn't do things without telling

1084
00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:15.620
Pam, she'd be married to Roy.

1085
00:50:15.960 --> 00:50:18.540
I need to share how upset this has always made me.

1086
00:50:18.540 --> 00:50:23.200
Tanisha goes on to say, Pam chose to break up with Roy.

1087
00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:27.020
Jim had already left Scranton to work at the Stanford branch.

1088
00:50:27.360 --> 00:50:31.640
Pam broke off the engagement with Roy because she realized it wasn't what she wanted.

1089
00:50:32.300 --> 00:50:33.960
Then she got a new apartment.

1090
00:50:34.240 --> 00:50:35.460
She started art classes.

1091
00:50:35.780 --> 00:50:38.060
Her art was included in an art exhibition.

1092
00:50:38.260 --> 00:50:43.440
She did the coal walk in the beach games episode and so much more character growth throughout

1093
00:50:43.440 --> 00:50:50.180
the series, all of which she did without Jim, all at a time when Jim wasn't even being friendly

1094
00:50:50.180 --> 00:50:50.940
with her anymore.

1095
00:50:51.140 --> 00:50:56.380
So I just think that Jim saying that Pam would be married to Roy completely disregards all of

1096
00:50:56.380 --> 00:50:57.240
Pam's accomplishments.

1097
00:50:57.240 --> 00:51:00.240
And it felt like a very un-Jim thing for him to have said.

1098
00:51:02.430 --> 00:51:03.430
I liked it.

1099
00:51:03.430 --> 00:51:04.530
I liked that.

1100
00:51:04.730 --> 00:51:05.650
That is fantastic.

1101
00:51:05.650 --> 00:51:07.390
I love that observation.

1102
00:51:08.310 --> 00:51:12.830
Well, ultimately, Jim tells Toby, you know, if Pam could just hold on a little bit longer,

1103
00:51:12.830 --> 00:51:14.950
this would be huge for our family.

1104
00:51:15.630 --> 00:51:19.170
And then Toby says, well, what does a little while mean?

1105
00:51:19.630 --> 00:51:25.190
Because, you know, for Pam to sign on to something that makes her unhappy, she's going to need

1106
00:51:25.190 --> 00:51:26.650
to know, is there an end date?

1107
00:51:27.770 --> 00:51:30.690
And Jim says, well, that's a pretty impossible question.

1108
00:51:31.170 --> 00:51:31.870
Mm-hmm.

1109
00:51:32.610 --> 00:51:38.670
So after the table read of this episode, when we were doing our kind of note session,

1110
00:51:39.590 --> 00:51:41.950
Greg explained his goal for these scenes.

1111
00:51:42.230 --> 00:51:48.110
He said that he wanted them to be shot and cut in such a way that it would look like Jim

1112
00:51:48.110 --> 00:51:49.750
and Pam were talking to each other.

1113
00:51:50.770 --> 00:51:55.370
Since we couldn't actually have cameras go into their couple's counseling, this was our version

1114
00:51:55.370 --> 00:51:57.450
of being able to see what they might say there.

1115
00:51:57.590 --> 00:51:59.690
Right, a sneak peek into what's going on.

1116
00:51:59.690 --> 00:52:00.070
Mm-hmm.

1117
00:52:00.150 --> 00:52:05.730
Greg said it was also really important that Toby take this seriously and that Nellie take

1118
00:52:05.730 --> 00:52:06.370
it seriously.

1119
00:52:06.990 --> 00:52:12.630
And John Krasinski also gave the note that Toby and Nellie should challenge us in some way.

1120
00:52:12.750 --> 00:52:16.170
Because originally, those kind of challenges from Nellie and Toby, they weren't in the table

1121
00:52:16.170 --> 00:52:16.550
draft.

1122
00:52:16.690 --> 00:52:17.590
So those got added.

1123
00:52:17.750 --> 00:52:19.090
I thought that was a really good thing.

1124
00:52:19.750 --> 00:52:23.970
You know, John had pitched that Toby should say something like, how do you think this makes

1125
00:52:23.970 --> 00:52:24.590
Pam feel?

1126
00:52:24.790 --> 00:52:28.970
And maybe Nellie should say, I don't understand why you don't just go.

1127
00:52:29.150 --> 00:52:30.110
Just go.

1128
00:52:30.370 --> 00:52:37.090
So that they're kind of being forced to address these things that are stopping them from getting

1129
00:52:37.090 --> 00:52:38.390
through this rough patch.

1130
00:52:38.570 --> 00:52:38.730
Yeah.

1131
00:52:39.110 --> 00:52:40.590
And I really liked this.

1132
00:52:40.750 --> 00:52:42.710
I liked this beat for their relationship.

1133
00:52:42.990 --> 00:52:43.690
I did too.

1134
00:52:43.690 --> 00:52:49.750
I did read in the shooting draft that it said Nellie is so invested that when Pam is saying

1135
00:52:49.750 --> 00:52:57.150
all these things that she wants to change, Nellie loses herself in Pam's speech and then

1136
00:52:57.150 --> 00:52:59.270
just says, I will change, Pam.

1137
00:52:59.610 --> 00:53:00.730
And then she's like, oh, wait.

1138
00:53:03.550 --> 00:53:09.510
Well, now Erin is going to call everyone over to watch Senator Lipton's press conference

1139
00:53:09.510 --> 00:53:10.930
on her computer.

1140
00:53:11.990 --> 00:53:13.790
Angela's on the TV and she looks real good.

1141
00:53:13.990 --> 00:53:15.650
They say she looks like a movie star.

1142
00:53:16.130 --> 00:53:21.190
This, of course, pisses Andy off because he thinks Angela's having her big TV moment that

1143
00:53:21.190 --> 00:53:22.210
he should be having.

1144
00:53:22.810 --> 00:53:27.070
But this press conference is not going to go the way Angela had hoped.

1145
00:53:27.070 --> 00:53:28.510
No, not at all.

1146
00:53:28.690 --> 00:53:32.830
Robert starts off by saying, I would just like to start by saying there have been some

1147
00:53:32.830 --> 00:53:36.410
rumors about my personal life that I would like to settle once and for all.

1148
00:53:36.590 --> 00:53:40.510
As my long-suffering wife can attest, I am gay.

1149
00:53:41.290 --> 00:53:43.250
The crowd reacts surprised.

1150
00:53:43.570 --> 00:53:48.110
And a reporter says, Senator, were you always gay or did your wife turn you gay?

1151
00:53:48.390 --> 00:53:51.670
And then the next reporter says, question for the senator's beard.

1152
00:53:52.730 --> 00:53:54.110
Pete's like, poor Angela.

1153
00:53:54.890 --> 00:53:56.950
Phyllis is sort of enjoying this moment.

1154
00:53:57.070 --> 00:53:59.150
She's like, yeah, poor Angela.

1155
00:53:59.590 --> 00:54:01.130
The senator is not done.

1156
00:54:01.350 --> 00:54:05.110
He's going to double down and say, I once believed that a gay person could be somewhat

1157
00:54:05.110 --> 00:54:05.510
straight.

1158
00:54:05.830 --> 00:54:11.690
It wasn't until my marriage to Angela that I realized how charmless I find the female body.

1159
00:54:12.370 --> 00:54:15.050
Meredith says, oof, always hurts to hear that one.

1160
00:54:16.750 --> 00:54:19.610
Robert is then going to go on to thank Oscar.

1161
00:54:19.930 --> 00:54:21.450
Oh, Oscar lights up.

1162
00:54:21.630 --> 00:54:22.790
He's so delighted.

1163
00:54:22.790 --> 00:54:29.150
And that's when the bullpen finds out that Oscar was also seeing the senator.

1164
00:54:29.950 --> 00:54:36.090
Let me tell you, Brian's performance as Kevin in this moment is absolutely amazing.

1165
00:54:36.090 --> 00:54:37.730
And I think we should hear it.

1166
00:54:38.910 --> 00:54:39.150
Yes.

1167
00:54:39.490 --> 00:54:41.970
And I knew it the whole time.

1168
00:54:42.190 --> 00:54:43.390
I kept a secret.

1169
00:54:43.710 --> 00:54:44.690
I kept a secret.

1170
00:54:44.870 --> 00:54:45.930
So good.

1171
00:54:46.250 --> 00:54:47.450
You didn't know.

1172
00:54:47.610 --> 00:54:48.710
You didn't know.

1173
00:54:48.910 --> 00:54:50.510
You didn't freaking know.

1174
00:54:50.670 --> 00:54:52.270
But I knew.

1175
00:54:52.490 --> 00:54:52.970
He knew.

1176
00:54:53.130 --> 00:54:53.610
Yes.

1177
00:54:53.750 --> 00:54:54.590
We did it.

1178
00:54:54.730 --> 00:54:55.630
You did it, Kevin.

1179
00:54:56.290 --> 00:54:56.790
Yes.

1180
00:54:57.750 --> 00:55:00.150
Oh, I did it.

1181
00:55:00.810 --> 00:55:02.490
Oh, I did it.

1182
00:55:04.630 --> 00:55:08.830
That is one of my best, like, slow play jokes.

1183
00:55:09.050 --> 00:55:11.170
He has known for so long.

1184
00:55:11.170 --> 00:55:12.290
So long.

1185
00:55:12.450 --> 00:55:13.490
And he did it.

1186
00:55:13.670 --> 00:55:14.590
He did it.

1187
00:55:14.710 --> 00:55:16.870
He kept that secret so good.

1188
00:55:18.450 --> 00:55:20.870
Well, this press conference scene was much longer.

1189
00:55:21.070 --> 00:55:28.430
Robert's going to go on to say that Oscar is who really opened up his eyes to his true love, Wesley.

1190
00:55:28.750 --> 00:55:32.990
Yes, his amazing chief of staff who he is in love with.

1191
00:55:33.130 --> 00:55:33.530
Yes.

1192
00:55:33.750 --> 00:55:34.850
And they kiss.

1193
00:55:35.270 --> 00:55:36.530
There was even more.

1194
00:55:36.810 --> 00:55:38.390
It's in the deleted scenes.

1195
00:55:38.750 --> 00:55:42.090
Angela is still going to try to salvage her marriage.

1196
00:55:42.090 --> 00:55:45.410
But the senator has more surprises in store for her.

1197
00:55:45.730 --> 00:55:46.910
You got to hear this.

1198
00:55:47.650 --> 00:55:50.950
Senator Lipton, what effect will this have on your family arrangement?

1199
00:55:51.270 --> 00:55:53.730
Will you and Mrs. Lipton continue living together?

1200
00:55:54.330 --> 00:55:55.050
Of course.

1201
00:55:55.270 --> 00:55:57.250
As you know, Robert and I have a young child.

1202
00:55:57.290 --> 00:55:58.550
I'll be moving in with Wesley.

1203
00:55:59.370 --> 00:55:59.810
What?

1204
00:55:59.970 --> 00:56:02.110
We need to know who we are as a couple.

1205
00:56:02.450 --> 00:56:04.230
Robert, we never discussed this.

1206
00:56:04.370 --> 00:56:09.110
Will you take this opportunity to promote change in the Republican Party with respect to their stance on gay marriage?

1207
00:56:09.530 --> 00:56:11.610
No, I'll just be switching to the Democratic Party.

1208
00:56:16.980 --> 00:56:18.900
Angela is so mortified at the end.

1209
00:56:19.120 --> 00:56:27.440
And I remember talking to Matt Sohn at the end of the scene because they had me just standing there through the whole thing as the senator just keeps dropping one bomb after another.

1210
00:56:27.800 --> 00:56:31.420
And I said, Matt, she's got to have a breaking point at some point.

1211
00:56:31.600 --> 00:56:33.200
Like, she wouldn't just keep standing there.

1212
00:56:33.480 --> 00:56:40.680
So at the end, when he says he's going to switch to the Democratic Party, Matt said, do whatever you think Angela would do in this moment.

1213
00:56:40.840 --> 00:56:41.080
Okay.

1214
00:56:41.400 --> 00:56:44.940
I shoved the microphone stand over and I stormed off the stage.

1215
00:56:44.940 --> 00:56:46.060
That was the last straw.

1216
00:56:46.300 --> 00:56:46.460
Yeah.

1217
00:56:46.760 --> 00:56:47.980
All this other stuff.

1218
00:56:48.160 --> 00:56:48.520
Right.

1219
00:56:48.680 --> 00:56:49.380
But not that.

1220
00:56:49.560 --> 00:56:50.160
Not that.

1221
00:56:51.580 --> 00:56:54.580
Well, you guys, Wesley Silver was played by Fred Cross.

1222
00:56:54.800 --> 00:56:58.120
He's a comedian and improv actor and graduate of the Second City.

1223
00:56:58.300 --> 00:57:00.580
And we've heard about him for a while now.

1224
00:57:01.360 --> 00:57:02.940
But boy, this was a shocker.

1225
00:57:03.220 --> 00:57:03.420
Yeah.

1226
00:57:03.420 --> 00:57:10.380
All of this betrayal of both Angela and Oscar is going to lead to some pretty fun stuff coming up in these next few episodes.

1227
00:57:10.860 --> 00:57:14.220
Yes, because they used to have a common love, but now they're going to have a common enemy.

1228
00:57:14.580 --> 00:57:14.920
Mm-hmm.

1229
00:57:14.920 --> 00:57:17.300
Up next is such a hilarious scene.

1230
00:57:17.760 --> 00:57:21.000
Andy has found what he hopes to be his new agent.

1231
00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:22.660
It's Carla Fern.

1232
00:57:23.240 --> 00:57:26.160
This is how this scene is described in the shooting draft.

1233
00:57:26.160 --> 00:57:35.900
It says Andy sits in a very narrow, bare-bones waiting room, some chairs, a dingy-looking water cooler, and a very plastic plant.

1234
00:57:36.260 --> 00:57:42.400
A mom reads a magazine as her freckled-faced redhead son, dressed in a jacket and tie, plays with her phone.

1235
00:57:42.700 --> 00:57:50.460
Andy looks across the waiting room where a clean-cut man sits next to a live dog with a live cat on top,

1236
00:57:50.460 --> 00:57:53.400
and the cat has an alive mouse on top of it.

1237
00:57:54.180 --> 00:57:54.660
Yes.

1238
00:57:55.420 --> 00:57:59.740
Andy is going to spot this man and say, are you an actor?

1239
00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:03.120
And he says, I'm not an actor, but I have an act.

1240
00:58:03.260 --> 00:58:04.940
It's dog-cat-mouse.

1241
00:58:05.880 --> 00:58:08.820
Andy says, is it hard to train them to do that?

1242
00:58:09.480 --> 00:58:12.940
And the man says, well, I've gone through a lot of mice.

1243
00:58:13.500 --> 00:58:15.980
We got a fan question from Abilene R.

1244
00:58:16.040 --> 00:58:17.880
in Chattanooga, Tennessee, who said,

1245
00:58:17.880 --> 00:58:22.200
oh, my God, is that Paul Feig at the casting office with the dog, cat, and mouse?

1246
00:58:22.340 --> 00:58:23.440
He's so funny.

1247
00:58:23.760 --> 00:58:26.040
His talking head delivery was amazing.

1248
00:58:26.220 --> 00:58:27.340
How did this come about?

1249
00:58:28.140 --> 00:58:31.680
Yes, Abilene, this was Paul Feig.

1250
00:58:32.120 --> 00:58:34.840
Greg wanted Paul to play this role.

1251
00:58:35.620 --> 00:58:38.080
And he is so, so funny.

1252
00:58:38.260 --> 00:58:39.520
He's so funny.

1253
00:58:39.720 --> 00:58:44.220
I thought we could hear his talking head, and then I would share the candy bag alts.

1254
00:58:44.340 --> 00:58:45.140
I love it.

1255
00:58:45.420 --> 00:58:47.680
Is it hard to train them to do that?

1256
00:58:48.500 --> 00:58:49.760
You go through a lot of mice.

1257
00:58:50.060 --> 00:58:52.840
It started by accident, actually, as these things tend to do.

1258
00:58:52.980 --> 00:58:55.680
I was sending out my cat, and I accidentally put her on top of my dog,

1259
00:58:55.840 --> 00:58:57.680
and I was so mad at myself at firSt.

1260
00:58:57.800 --> 00:59:00.560
And I was like, wait, wait a second.

1261
00:59:04.640 --> 00:59:08.640
So that talking head was actually the candy bag alt.

1262
00:59:09.040 --> 00:59:09.480
Yes.

1263
00:59:09.920 --> 00:59:11.800
And he would have gone on to say,

1264
00:59:12.120 --> 00:59:14.800
my ex-wife tells everyone the mouse was her idea,

1265
00:59:14.800 --> 00:59:16.960
but I don't have any recollection of that.

1266
00:59:17.420 --> 00:59:19.220
Anyway, I would have thought of it myself.

1267
00:59:19.860 --> 00:59:22.160
And here were the other talking head options.

1268
00:59:22.960 --> 00:59:25.200
I've done birthdays, retirement parties.

1269
00:59:25.220 --> 00:59:27.060
I did a shoe store opening once.

1270
00:59:27.220 --> 00:59:32.040
I still try to live off just the money I get from performing in parking lots behind Cvs.

1271
00:59:32.040 --> 00:59:36.880
And then everything beyond that is bonus, because you don't know when this could all go away.

1272
00:59:37.980 --> 00:59:39.040
Here's another one.

1273
00:59:39.460 --> 00:59:45.820
I guess my realistic dream is to do a horse, dog, cat, mouse, all four in a column, you know?

1274
00:59:46.560 --> 00:59:55.160
And then sometimes, and I know how this sounds, but I imagine doing all four of those with like a piece of cheese on top of the mouse's hat.

1275
00:59:55.480 --> 00:59:59.680
You wouldn't think the cheese is the hardest part, but all of them love cheese.

1276
01:00:00.360 --> 01:00:04.480
Well, Andy does ask, does anything go on top of the mouse?

1277
01:00:04.980 --> 01:00:07.040
And he says, yes, a little hat.

1278
01:00:07.680 --> 01:00:10.300
