1324
01:02:37.980 --> 01:02:41.780
There is a very famous dog cat mouse man from Bisbee, Arizona.

1325
01:02:42.340 --> 01:02:42.800
Oh.

1326
01:02:42.800 --> 01:02:52.800
His name is Greg Pike, and he will set up on a sidewalk with his dog cat mouse, and he'll accept donations from anyone who wants to take a picture with them.

1327
01:02:53.020 --> 01:02:54.980
His dog's name is Booger.

1328
01:02:55.220 --> 01:02:56.900
His cat's name is Kitty.

1329
01:02:57.120 --> 01:02:58.960
And his mouse's name is Mousy.

1330
01:02:59.060 --> 01:03:00.300
And they all stand together.

1331
01:03:00.300 --> 01:03:03.000
So this might have been the inspiration.

1332
01:03:03.260 --> 01:03:03.660
Got it.

1333
01:03:03.660 --> 01:03:04.880
And the town loves him.

1334
01:03:05.160 --> 01:03:07.360
The town loves Greg and his animal stack.

1335
01:03:07.920 --> 01:03:13.420
Steve said that Bob Dunn's initially offered a stack of dog cat rat.

1336
01:03:14.120 --> 01:03:16.940
Rats are very smart, and they're very easy to train.

1337
01:03:17.280 --> 01:03:24.360
They sent over a picture of what a dog cat rat might look like, and the writers really wanted a mouse.

1338
01:03:25.240 --> 01:03:25.720
Yeah.

1339
01:03:26.160 --> 01:03:27.160
I don't know.

1340
01:03:27.340 --> 01:03:29.220
Like, a mouse is so tiny.

1341
01:03:29.460 --> 01:03:30.240
It kind of like—

1342
01:03:30.240 --> 01:03:32.160
And then the little top hat's even tinier.

1343
01:03:32.260 --> 01:03:33.240
It's even so smaller.

1344
01:03:33.460 --> 01:03:33.580
Yeah.

1345
01:03:34.200 --> 01:03:39.620
Well, Steve wrote to them and said, hey, we would really like to try for a mouse on top of the cat.

1346
01:03:40.020 --> 01:03:43.740
The rat could be a backup, but our first choice is mouse.

1347
01:03:44.080 --> 01:03:45.880
These are the emails Steve Burgess writes.

1348
01:03:46.160 --> 01:03:46.300
Mm-hmm.

1349
01:03:46.680 --> 01:03:50.060
Bob Dunn wrote back, Steve, mouse's first choice.

1350
01:03:50.260 --> 01:03:51.220
We'll let the mouse know.

1351
01:03:52.060 --> 01:03:53.240
That's what he wrote back.

1352
01:03:54.400 --> 01:04:00.460
Well, Ange, I have even more information to share because remember when I did that AT&T commercial

1353
01:04:00.460 --> 01:04:03.400
and there was that pug from Bob Dunn's?

1354
01:04:03.640 --> 01:04:07.240
I told them, I am going to reach out to you for this podcaSt.

1355
01:04:07.720 --> 01:04:08.880
Well, I reached out.

1356
01:04:09.380 --> 01:04:11.840
I said, how did you stack these animals?

1357
01:04:12.780 --> 01:04:18.400
Well, Mike Morris, the head animal trainer at Bob Dunn's, wrote me back and said this.

1358
01:04:18.980 --> 01:04:19.500
Hello.

1359
01:04:19.500 --> 01:04:24.260
Oh, even though I didn't do this job myself, I remember it vividly because it was such a

1360
01:04:24.260 --> 01:04:28.360
funny scene and it was really fun for the trainers to prep and I was around while they

1361
01:04:28.360 --> 01:04:29.700
were prepping for the shoot day.

1362
01:04:30.140 --> 01:04:35.640
He said, many times productions come up with an idea that requires a specific look or an

1363
01:04:35.640 --> 01:04:40.300
exact animal type and other times they have specific action requirements.

1364
01:04:40.300 --> 01:04:45.660
He said, we did not have animals who were ready to be stacked, but what we did have were animals

1365
01:04:45.660 --> 01:04:49.160
that were continually being trained to learn new behaviors as needed.

1366
01:04:49.900 --> 01:04:52.220
And a lot of the basics are the same.

1367
01:04:52.460 --> 01:04:58.540
So in order to do this particular scene, he said, we would have looked for a veteran animal

1368
01:04:58.540 --> 01:05:02.000
cat and dog, really well-trained, calm animals.

1369
01:05:02.000 --> 01:05:04.520
He said it took about three days to prep.

1370
01:05:04.780 --> 01:05:10.060
He said the mice, they had to start from scratch and it would start with a group of mice and

1371
01:05:10.060 --> 01:05:13.540
people would just handle them and get them really comfortable around people.

1372
01:05:13.800 --> 01:05:18.860
And they would prep multiple mice and ultimately they selected the one that was the most comfortable

1373
01:05:18.860 --> 01:05:19.900
on the shoot day.

1374
01:05:20.340 --> 01:05:22.140
He said they prepped them in groups.

1375
01:05:22.360 --> 01:05:27.580
They did a mouse cat prep and a cat dog prep, and then they would bring them all together.

1376
01:05:27.580 --> 01:05:35.860
Steve Burgess added that they specifically chose animals with contrasting fur colors so that

1377
01:05:35.860 --> 01:05:36.940
they would show up more.

1378
01:05:37.100 --> 01:05:41.340
I guess the original video that they had sent, the animals were kind of the same color, so

1379
01:05:41.340 --> 01:05:42.620
they were harder to distinguish.

1380
01:05:43.000 --> 01:05:44.140
They got a pop on TV.

1381
01:05:44.480 --> 01:05:45.680
Yeah, that's right.

1382
01:05:46.300 --> 01:05:52.100
They also put a harness on the dog so that when the cat sat on top of the dog, the cat's

1383
01:05:52.100 --> 01:05:54.620
claws wouldn't dig into the dog in any way.

1384
01:05:54.740 --> 01:05:55.540
Yeah, that's smart.

1385
01:05:55.540 --> 01:05:58.900
He said you couldn't see the harness because the dog's fur covered it up.

1386
01:05:59.500 --> 01:06:03.720
Steve said the hardest part was getting the mouse to stay on top of the cat, as you could

1387
01:06:03.720 --> 01:06:04.040
imagine.

1388
01:06:04.800 --> 01:06:09.660
And the total cost for this animal training and this shoot day was around $10,000.

1389
01:06:10.440 --> 01:06:11.900
Well, they pulled it off.

1390
01:06:12.040 --> 01:06:13.660
I mean, it's hilarious.

1391
01:06:13.660 --> 01:06:17.980
And we asked Matt Stone about all of this, about getting to work with Paul Feig, and then

1392
01:06:17.980 --> 01:06:19.820
about the whole stacking scene.

1393
01:06:20.020 --> 01:06:21.160
Here's what he had to say.

1394
01:06:21.740 --> 01:06:24.700
I was very excited to get to direct Paul.

1395
01:06:24.700 --> 01:06:30.260
It was also really fun because we got to pick out this suit for him.

1396
01:06:30.970 --> 01:06:36.700
And for those who don't know, Paul always wears the most fashionable suits.

1397
01:06:37.100 --> 01:06:45.980
And it was fun to find this corduroy suit that our wardrobe department found and put him

1398
01:06:45.980 --> 01:06:46.220
in.

1399
01:06:46.420 --> 01:06:47.240
He looked great.

1400
01:06:47.240 --> 01:06:51.780
So he had his act, the dog-cat-mouse.

1401
01:06:52.100 --> 01:06:55.500
I know that Paul had a lot of fun with the scene.

1402
01:06:55.660 --> 01:07:02.960
And it took us a little while to shoot because we had to get the right angle that sold all

1403
01:07:02.960 --> 01:07:08.080
of these moments, because the mouse was so small, the dog was so big.

1404
01:07:08.400 --> 01:07:10.480
Of course, the cat was right there in between.

1405
01:07:11.260 --> 01:07:15.260
But it all ultimately worked out well and was very funny.

1406
01:07:15.260 --> 01:07:20.860
And the final moment with the cat was sort of a happy accident.

1407
01:07:21.320 --> 01:07:28.920
You know, we were shooting every bit of the moment of the mouse staying on top of the

1408
01:07:28.920 --> 01:07:35.800
cat, on top of the dog, so we could have as many options to choose from in the edit.

1409
01:07:35.800 --> 01:07:44.160
And, you know, it truly was that happy accident that the cat jumped off of the dog and the mouse

1410
01:07:44.160 --> 01:07:47.260
stayed on the cat's back.

1411
01:07:47.620 --> 01:07:55.920
And then I think the end of the episode is also very funny in that the cat is just chewing

1412
01:07:55.920 --> 01:08:03.300
